## Why This Is Asked

This question probes the ability to design a robust cross-namespace orchestration primitive, with DAG scheduling, idempotent reconciliation, and cleanup semantics. It also touches RBAC, metrics, and testing at scale.

## Key Concepts

- CustomResourceDefinition and controller patterns
- DAG scheduling and dependency resolution
- Per-namespace isolation and quotas
- Idempotent reconciler design
- Finalizers and cleanup lifecycle
- Observability: conditions, events, metrics across namespaces
- Backoff strategies with jitter

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo reconciler structure
async function reconcile(dataMigrationPlan) {
  // Parse DAG and identify ready tasks
  const readyTasks = parseDAG(dataMigrationPlan.spec.tasks);

  for (const task of readyTasks) {
    // Check idempotency
    if (await jobExists(task.name)) {
      continue;
    }

    // Create Job with backoff strategy
    await createJob(task);

    // Add finalizer for cleanup
    await addFinalizer(dataMigrationPlan, task.name);
  }

  // Update status and conditions
  await updateStatus(dataMigrationPlan);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design Kubernetes-native controls for multi-tenant, CI-heavy workloads, focusing on per-namespace isolation, clean lifecycle management with finalizers, and comprehensive observability.

## Key Concepts

- CustomResourceDefinition and controller reconcile loop patterns
- Per-namespace NetworkPolicy generation and cleanup automation
- Finalizers guaranteeing cleanup after policy removal
- Observability through status fields and Prometheus metrics
- Multi-tenant security isolation in shared Kubernetes environments

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-reconcile flow for EphemeralTenantPolicy
async function reconcile(policy) {
  if (!policy.finalizers.includes('ephemeral.cleanup')) {
    // Add finalizer to ensure cleanup
    policy.finalizers.push('ephemeral.cleanup');
  }

  // Create or update NetworkPolicy for CI isolation
  const networkPolicy = generateNetworkPolicy(policy);
  await applyNetworkPolicy(networkPolicy);

  // Update status and emit metrics
  updatePolicyStatus(policy, 'Active');
  emitMetrics('policy_reconciled', policy.name);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Advanced latency management in multi-tenant clusters requires concrete CRD-driven control with observable outcomes and safe rollback.

## Key Concepts
- Kubernetes CRD and controller reconciliation loops
- Prometheus-based latency metrics (p95) and windowing
- Mitigations: HPA scaling, admission throttling, proxy-sidecar rate limits
- Status, events, auditability, and rollback semantics

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: reconciliation skeleton
function reconcile(spec, status) {
  const latency = queryPrometheus(spec.namespace, spec.serviceSelector, 'p95');
  if (latency > spec.budgetMs) {
    scaleOut(spec.namespace, spec.serviceSelector);
    enableRateLimit(spec.sidecarSvc, spec.budgetMs);
  } else {
    removeRateLimit(spec.sidecarSvc);
  }
  return { observedLatency: latency, breached: latency > spec.budgetMs };
}
```

